                  "kind": "text",
                  "data": "\n\nAll three remarks are valid. Let me address each:\n\n1. **Data leakage in BERT** — You're absolutely right. The current code trains fold after fold on the same model instance, so weights from fold 1 carry into fold 2, etc. Each fold needs a freshly initialized model to be a proper independent evaluation. This is a significant issue.\n\n2. **Inconsistency in label extraction** — For SVM/baseline, `_extract_texts_and_labels` builds labels from `config.dataset_classes` + derives `not_hate` from `hate`. For BERT, the labels come from `train_tokenized` (which was tokenized by the Model class, also building the 12-class vector). The logic is the same but the code paths differ. I'll unify them.\n\n3. **Model saving** — Saving only the last fold's model is meaningless for evaluation purposes. For a publication, cross-validation is for metric estimation, not for producing a final model. I'll remove the save-after-CV logic. If you want a final model for inference/XAI, that should be a separate full-training step (no CV), which we can discuss later.\n\nBefore I proceed, one clarification on point 1:\n\nFor reinitializing the BERT model each fold — this means I need to re-download/reload the pretrained weights, re-create the Trainer, etc. for every fold. The current `Model` class does this in `__init__`. Should I:\n- (A) Create a fresh `Model` instance each fold (simplest, but means repeated loading from disk/HuggingFace), or\n- (B) Add a `reset()` method to the Model class that reloads the pretrained weights without re-downloading?\n\nAlso for the **new stratification function** — you want to stratify by the single most prevalent class per sample (e.g., if a sample has `racism=1, xenophobia=1`, pick one dominant class)? Or do you mean something else by \"stratify by class and not multilabel combinations\"? For instance, do you want to use `IterativeStratification` from `skmultilearn` which considers each label independently when distributing across folds?"
                }
