              "content": [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "data": "\n\n**On `reset()`**: Yes, a `reset()` method that reloads the pretrained weights from the original checkpoint (either from HuggingFace or from the local cached download — *not* from `fine-tuned-models/`) would guarantee no data leakage. The key is that:\n- The model weights are reset to the pretrained state (no fine-tuning information from previous folds)\n- The optimizer/scheduler state inside the Trainer is also recreated fresh (which happens automatically when you call `Trainer.train()` on a new Trainer instance or reassign the model)\n\nSo concretely, `reset()` would:\n1. Reload `self.model` from `self.link` (the original pretrained checkpoint)\n2. Move it to GPU\n3. Recreate the Trainer with the fresh model\n\nThis is equivalent to creating a new `Model` instance but avoids re-downloading the tokenizer (which doesn't change) and re-tokenizing the dataset.\n\n---\n\n**On Iterative Stratification**:\n\nYour current dataset is multilabel — a single sample can have multiple classes active (e.g., `racism=1, xenophobia=1, misogyny=1`). The approach I already implemented (label-combination) treats each *unique combination* as a single class (e.g., \"racism+xenophobia+misogyny\" is one stratum). This preserves the exact co-occurrence patterns, but:\n- It creates many strata (potentially hundreds of unique combos)\n- Rare combos get merged into a \"rare\" bucket, losing stratification precision\n\n**Iterative Stratification** (from `skmultilearn.model_selection.IterativeStratification`) works differently:\n- It considers each label *independently* and tries to ensure that each fold has approximately the same proportion of positive samples for *every* label\n- It iteratively assigns samples to folds, prioritizing the rarest labels first to ensure they're well-distributed\n- It doesn't care about combinations — it optimizes the marginal distribution of each class across folds\n\n**Practical difference**: \n- Label-combination: \"each fold has the same proportion of samples that are *exactly* racism+xenophobia\"\n- Iterative: \"each fold has roughly the same % of racism samples, AND the same % of xenophobia samples, AND the same % of misogyny samples, etc. — independently\"\n\nIterative stratification is generally preferred for multilabel problems in the literature because it handles rare labels better and doesn't suffer from the combinatorial explosion of unique label sets.\n\nWould you like me to use `IterativeStratification` from `skmultilearn` (which is already in your dependencies) for this new function?"
                }
              ],
